@misc{categorytheorycontext,
  title        = {{Category Theory in Context}},
  key          = {Category Theory in Context},
  url          = {https://emilyriehl.github.io/files/context.pdf},
  howpublished = {\url{https://emilyriehl.github.io/files/context.pdf}},
  keywords     = {math.CT, mathematics, category-theory, enriched-and-formal-category-theory, mathematical-logic, point-set-topology, book, github},
  abstract     = {Category Theory in Context. Emily Riehl's foundational textbook covering: - **Preservation, Reflection, Creation:** Functors preserve limits (limit in domain $\implies$ limit in codomain), reflect limits (limit cone in image $\implies$ limit cone in domain), and create limits (limit in codomain $\implies$ unique limit lifting). - **Kan Extensions:** Left Kan extensions $\operatorname{Lan}_K F$ (generalizing colimits) and right Kan extensions $\operatorname{Ran}_K F$ (generalizing limits). Pointwise Kan extensions are computed via limit formulas over comma categories: $\operatorname{Ran}_K F(d) \cong \lim (d/K \to \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{E})$. - **Yoneda \& Density:** The Yoneda lemma as a pointwise Kan extension. The co-Yoneda lemma expresses every presheaf as a colimit of representables: $F(c) \cong \operatorname{colim}(\mathcal{C}/c \to \mathcal{C} \to \mathbf{Set})$. - **Codensity Monads:** The right Kan extension of a functor along itself. The codensity monad of $\mathbf{FinSet} \hookrightarrow \mathbf{Set}$ is the ultrafilter monad.}
}

