  keywords     = {math.CO, math.QA, hep-th, mathematics, physics, graph-theory, vertex-algebras, talk},
  abstract     = {Michael Brannan (joint with Kari Eifler, Christian Voigt, and Moritz Weber), quantum graphs admit universal quantum Cuntz--Krieger algebras that recover the classical construction for commutative vertex algebras and exhibit new nonnuclear examples. Key computation: for $\mathcal G=(B,\psi,A)$ with $B=\bigoplus_{k=1}^mM_{n(k)}(\mathbb C)$, writing $S^{(k)}=(\dim B/n(k))[S(e_{ij}^{(k)})]$ turns the defining relation $\mu(1\otimes\mu)(S\otimes S^*\otimes S)(1\otimes m^*)m^*=S$ into $S^{(k)}S^{(k)*}S^{(k)}=S^{(k)}$, while $\mu(S^*\otimes S)m^*=\mu(S\otimes S^*)m^*A$ becomes $S^{(k)*}S^{(k)}=(S^{(1)}S^{(1)*},\ldots,S^{(m)}S^{(m)*})\widetilde A$; these relations define $\mathcal O_{\mathcal G}$, give $\mathcal O_{\mathcal G}$ equal to the usual Cuntz--Krieger algebra when $B$ is abelian, and for the complete quantum graph yield the explicit quotient $\mathcal O_{K(M_n)}\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal O_{n^2}$ by $S_{ij}\mapsto n^{-1/2}\widehat S_{ij}$.}
}

